 "verdict": [
  "1. Row 11 does not test the framework's law.  The law's deep slope is 1/2 at both footings (M1); the rule n = 2*beta",
  "   assigns the canonical footing beta = 1, the Newtonian slope, which neither footing predicts.",
  "   On data that obey the law exactly, row 11 fires in 88 per cent of draws (per-galaxy reading) and 100 per cent (ring level).",
  "   I1 FAILED its pre-stated 95 per cent bar on the per-galaxy reading and stays FAIL.",
  "   With its own mapped null true (beta = 1) it fires in 4.5-7.0 / 7.8-14.5 per cent, and between the two footings its reading moves",
  "   0.009-0.010 against the 0.340 gap it claims to resolve (I2).  What fired is beta = 1, not n = 2.",
  "2. The measured deep slope agrees with the law's own slope at the same points (D1 PASS: pooled z +0.70, +0.39, +0.33, +0.37).",
  "   The data reject beta = 1 at 6.0-6.9 sigma but a slope of 0.75 only at 2.2-2.8 sigma: P1 FAILED as pre-stated.",
  "   The deep slope is consistent with the law; it is not yet a sharp confirmation of it.",
  "3. The ring-pooled slope sits 1.5-2.2 sigma BELOW the law's own expectation, inside the pre-stated 3 sigma (D2): a lean, not a failure.",
  "   Allowing 0.1 dex mass-to-light scatter moves z by at most 0.10.  On the committed corpus it sat 5.2 sigma below, the kinematic mass-to-light artefact of D01-D02.",
  "4. The footing question lives in the amplitude.  With the in-force kernel the three deep samples share one amplitude (A2: chi2 3.1-8.0, df 2).",
  "   Jointly n = 1.35-1.52 +/- 0.13-0.14: 3.3-4.8 sigma below the canonical n = 2.000 and 1.0-2.3 sigma below the alternative 1.660.",
  "   SPARC alone (population ratios): n = 1.61-1.97, within 0.1-1.6 sigma of 2.000.",
  "   MIGHTEE alone (the survey's own baryonic masses): n = 1.12 +/- 0.16, 5.3 sigma from 2.000 and 3.3 from 1.660, statistical errors only.",
  "   MIGHTEE's own refit at a SPARC-class Ystar_K = 0.6 gives a0 = 1.08e-10 = 1.15 a0_DE (Table 3 via G167); with MIGHTEE put there,",
  "   the joint becomes n = 1.66-1.83: 1.0-2.0 sigma from 2.000 and 0.0-1.0 from 1.660 (DIAGNOSIS 5, a what-if).",
  "   The deep amplitude sits between the two footings and is set by the stellar mass-to-light convention.  No sample sits above the n = 2.01 subluminality line.",
  "5. G158's '12.7 sigma' is a t statistic with 2 degrees of freedom: p = 0.0091, 2.6 sigma Gaussian-equivalent (DIAGNOSIS 1)."
 ]
}